{
 "id": "2021-ayugatellez-la",
 "name": "La Mancha Rural Landscapes: Visual Preference",
 "description": "Faithful replication of the photo questionnaire in Ayuga-Téllez, E., Ramírez-Montoro, J. J., Grande-Ortiz, M. Á., & Muñoz-Violero, D. (2021). Differences in visual preference in rural landscapes on the plain of La Mancha in Spain. Sustainability 13(24):13799. https://doi.org/10.3390/su132413799\n\nParticipants first answer the paper's profile questions (age, gender, university education or not, living in Campo de Criptana or elsewhere), then see the 16 photographs of the rural landscape of Campo de Criptana (Ciudad Real) one at a time in random order and rate each on the paper's scale: 1 = I don't like it at all, 2 = I don't like it much, 3 = I quite like it, 4 = I like it, 5 = I like it a lot. The photos cover the combinations of four attributes (fauna, vegetation, cultural resources such as windmills and shrines, breadth of the viewshed), listed in the paper's Table 1 with the same numbering (photo_01 … photo_16).\n\nOriginal study: 160 people in structured interviews (80 university and 80 non-university educated; locals and people from outside, interviewed in Madrid). The partridge in front of the shrine of La Virgen de Criptana (photo 9, mean 3.99) and the shrine among trees (photo 10, 3.88) scored highest and the grape-harvesting machine (photo 16, 2.68) lowest; educational level, gender and age all affected scores (ANOVA), with women, over-40s and non-university-educated people scoring higher on average.\n\nAnalysis as in the paper: mean scores per photo and per group, multifactor ANOVA on the mean score with the four profile variables, and Mann-Whitney tests per photo.\n\nDifferences from the original study: (1) online and self-paced instead of a structured interview with a printed photo album or laptop; (2) the paper reports age in two groups (under 40 / 40 and over), here age is asked in years so the same split can be made; (3) the profile question wording is ours, built from the paper's Table 2.\n\nImages: Supplementary Materials S1 of the open-access article (CC BY 4.0). Photos 1 (fox) and 7 (hare) came from a Spanish nature photography network, as credited in the paper. Shown with credit for research; the template is removed if the authors object.",
